questions.model_answer:
- **`assert property`** (§16.14.1): an obligation the **design** must meet. Simulation checks it on the traces the testbench produced and runs the fail action (default `$error`) when it fails. Formal tries to prove it for every input behaviour the assumptions allow, or returns a counterexample trace.
- **`assume property`** (§16.14.2): a rule the **environment** obeys. Formal treats it as a hypothesis: it considers only input behaviour that satisfies it and has no obligation to prove it. Simulation does not ignore it: an assumed property is checked and reported if it fails, exactly like an assertion, which tells you the testbench (or the real neighbour block) broke the rule. Some simulators can also use assumptions to generate stimulus (tool-dependent).
- **`cover property`** (§16.14.3): a scenario that must be **shown to happen**. Simulation counts attempts, successes (at most one per attempt) and vacuous successes. Formal searches for a trace that reaches it, or proves it unreachable (wording of results is tool-dependent).
- **`restrict property`** (§16.14.4): the same meaning as `assume` for formal, but simulation does not check it and it has no action block. It is for formal-only case splits such as "prove the ALU with `mode == 0`".

```systemverilog
property p_no_wr_when_full;
  @(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n) full |-> !wr_en;
endproperty

// In the FIFO's formal harness the producer is the environment:
asm_producer: assume property (p_no_wr_when_full);
// In the producer's harness the same rule is an obligation on that design:
ast_producer: assert property (p_no_wr_when_full);
// Proof that the interesting state was reached at all:
cov_fill:     cover  property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n) wr_en ##1 full);
```

The rule of thumb for a block harness: **assume on inputs, assert on outputs, cover the scenarios**. An assumption is a debt: it must be proven as an assertion somewhere else, or at least checked by the simulation regressions that run the real neighbour.

