questions.model_answer:
The rules come from Arm IHI0022E: once `VALID` is asserted it must remain asserted until the handshake (A3.2.1), the source must keep its information stable until the transfer (A3.2.1), and during reset a master must drive `ARVALID`, `AWVALID` and `WVALID` low (A3.1.2).

```systemverilog
// Placed in the AXI interface or a bound checker; all signals sampled at posedge ACLK.
property p_aw_valid_held;
  @(posedge ACLK) disable iff (!ARESETn)
    AWVALID && !AWREADY |=> AWVALID;
endproperty

property p_aw_payload_stable;
  @(posedge ACLK) disable iff (!ARESETn)
    AWVALID && !AWREADY |=> $stable({AWID, AWADDR, AWLEN, AWSIZE, AWBURST, AWLOCK, AWCACHE, AWPROT});
  // AXI4: add AWQOS, AWREGION and AWUSER when the interface has them;
  // A3.2.1 requires all information on the channel to stay stable.
endproperty

a_aw_valid_held: assert property (p_aw_valid_held)
  else $error("AWVALID dropped before AWREADY");
a_aw_stable:     assert property (p_aw_payload_stable)
  else $error("AW payload changed while stalled: AWADDR was %h, now %h", $past(AWADDR), $sampled(AWADDR));

// Reset rule: no disable iff here, the check is about reset itself.
// The first tick with ARESETn sampled high still shows the value driven during reset.
a_aw_reset: assert property (@(posedge ACLK) (!ARESETn || $rose(ARESETn)) |-> !AWVALID);

// Checker practice (not an IHI0022E rule): no X on control while out of reset.
a_aw_no_x: assert property (@(posedge ACLK) disable iff (!ARESETn)
  !$isunknown(AWVALID) && (AWVALID -> !$isunknown({AWADDR, AWLEN, AWSIZE, AWBURST})));

// Evidence the rules were exercised:
c_aw_stalled:     cover property (@(posedge ACLK) disable iff (!ARESETn) AWVALID && !AWREADY ##1 AWVALID && AWREADY);
c_aw_ready_first: cover property (@(posedge ACLK) disable iff (!ARESETn) !AWVALID && AWREADY ##1 AWVALID && AWREADY);
```

Notes a reviewer looks for:

- The antecedent `AWVALID && !AWREADY` is a stall; after a handshake (`AWVALID && AWREADY`) the master may change the payload or drop `AWVALID`, so those ticks are vacuous by design.
- `$stable` on the concatenation catches a change in any field but cannot say which one moved. For debuggable failures, write one `$stable` assertion per field (generated in a loop or a macro of your own), or print each field's `$past` and `$sampled` values in the message.
- There is **no** assertion that `AWREADY` must eventually rise. The slave can wait for `AWVALID` before asserting `AWREADY` (A3.3.1), and the specification sets no time limit. A latency bound such as `AWVALID |-> ##[0:16] AWREADY` is a requirement of a particular design, and should be labelled as such.
- In a formal harness of a slave, the master rules become `assume property`, and the slave-side rules become assertions.

They answer different questions.

- **Covergroup** (§19.3): *which values, and which combinations of values, did we see?* It samples at an event, sorts values into bins and crosses, and reports a percentage per bin. Use it for opcode × size × response, FIFO fill levels, register field values.
- **`cover property`** (§16.14.3): *did this ordered scenario happen?* It describes a pattern over cycles, for example a write to a full FIFO followed by a read in the next cycle, or back-to-back retries. In formal, the same cover is a reachability question: the tool finds a trace or proves it unreachable.

**Counting.** `cover sequence` reports **all matches** of each attempt. `cover property` increments its success count **at most once per attempt**, and reports separately the successes that were vacuous (§16.14.3). Disabled evaluations count as attempts but not as successes. That is why `cover property (req |-> ##[1:3] gnt)` is a mistake: the implication succeeds vacuously whenever `req` is low. Cover the sequence `req ##[1:3] gnt`, or use followed-by, `req #-# ##[1:3] gnt` (§16.12.9).

**Combining them: latency with bins.** A sequence can carry a local variable and call a void function at the end of a match (§16.10, §16.11), and a covergroup's `sample()` is a void function method (§19.8):

```systemverilog
// snippet
covergroup lat_cg with function sample(int unsigned lat);
  coverpoint lat { bins fast = {[1:2]}; bins mid = {[3:8]}; bins slow = {[9:16]}; }
endgroup
lat_cg lat_cov = new();

sequence s_req_to_gnt;
  int unsigned lat;
  @(posedge clk) ($rose(req), lat = 0)
    ##1 (!gnt, lat++)[*0:15]
    ##1 (gnt, lat++, lat_cov.sample(lat));
endsequence
c_req_to_gnt: cover sequence (s_req_to_gnt);
```

Each attempt keeps its own `lat` (local variables are per attempt), so overlapping requests do not corrupt each other. The cover tells you the scenario happened; the covergroup tells you which latencies happened.

Rule of thumb: values and combinations go in covergroups; orderings, overlaps and timing corner cases go in cover properties; and every assertion's antecedent gets a cover so a vacuous pass cannot hide.

```systemverilog
// Assertions packaged as a checker (Clause 17). Formal arguments default to input.
checker fifo_hs_chk (logic clk, logic rst_n, logic push, logic pop, logic full, logic empty);
  default clocking @(posedge clk); endclocking
  default disable iff (!rst_n);
  a_no_push_full:  assert property (push |-> !full);
  a_no_pop_empty:  assert property (pop  |-> !empty);
  c_fill_and_read: cover  property (push ##1 full ##[1:$] pop);
endchecker

checker fifo_cov_chk (logic clk, logic rst_n, logic [4:0] count);
  default clocking @(posedge clk); endclocking
  covergroup level_cg @(posedge clk);
    coverpoint count { bins empty = {0}; bins mid = {[1:15]}; bins full = {16}; }
  endgroup
  level_cg cg = new();
endchecker

// 1) Every instance of module fifo, design-wide.
//    wr_en, rd_en, full, empty are names INSIDE fifo, not in the scope of this bind.
bind fifo fifo_hs_chk u_hs_chk (.clk(clk), .rst_n(rst_n), .push(wr_en), .pop(rd_en),
                                .full(full), .empty(empty));

// 2) One specific instance only.
bind tb_top.dut.u_rx_fifo fifo_cov_chk u_cov_chk (.clk(clk), .rst_n(rst_n), .count(count));
```

**Name resolution.** All actual ports and parameters in a `bind` refer to objects from the viewpoint of the target: the bound instance behaves as if it were written at the very end of the target module, so it sees the target's declarations and imports. Names declared in the scope that contains the `bind` statement are not used, and declarations in `$unit` are not visible to the bind (§23.11). There is a third form, `bind fifo : tb_top.dut.u_rx_fifo, tb_top.dut.u_tx_fifo fifo_cov_chk u_cov_chk (...);`, for a list of instances of one module.

**Why a checker.** A checker can hold sequences, properties, assertions, covergroups, checker variables and `default clocking`/`default disable iff` (§17.2), and its arguments can be sequences or properties. It cannot declare or instantiate modules, interfaces or programs. A module or interface works too and is what many teams use for VIP-style monitors. If the bind target is an interface, the bound instance must be an interface or a checker (§23.11).

`bind` does not edit or recompile the RTL file; it adds an instance at elaboration, so the same checker can be bound in block simulation, SoC simulation and a formal harness.

