  asm_no_rd_empty: assume property (empty |-> !rd_en);
  asm_one_op:      assume property (!(wr_en && rd_en));
  asm_rd_with_wr:  assume property (wr_en |-> rd_en);   // "reads always follow writes"

  ast_full:  assert property (full == (count == DEPTH));
  ast_count: assert property (wr_en && !full |=> count == $past(count) + 1'b1);
endmodule

bind fifo fifo_props #(.DEPTH(4)) u_props (.*);
```

questions.model_answer:
**One byte in flight:**

```systemverilog
property p_data_one;
  logic [7:0] exp;                        // one copy per attempt
  @(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n)
    (in_vld, exp = in_data) |=> out_vld[->1] ##0 (out_data == exp);
endproperty
```

The match item `exp = in_data` runs when the antecedent matches and stores the sampled byte in this attempt's own local variable (§16.10). From the next tick, `out_vld[->1]` waits for the first `out_vld`, and `##0` compares `out_data` on that same tick. A module-level variable would be overwritten by the next input before the first byte comes out.

**Four in flight: it breaks.** Suppose bytes A and B enter at edges 2 and 3, and A leaves at edge 4. B's attempt starts waiting at edge 4 and its `out_vld[->1]` ends at A's handshake, so it compares A with B and fails, on a correct design. The property pairs each input with the **next** output, not with **its** output.

**Fix: a ticket.** Count accepted and delivered bytes; in an in-order pipeline, byte k enters when the input count is k and leaves when the output count is k.

```systemverilog
logic [2:0] in_cnt, out_cnt;   // 3 bits: safe while fewer than 8 bytes are in flight (max 4)
always_ff @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n)
  if (!rst_n) begin
    in_cnt  <= '0;
    out_cnt <= '0;
  end else begin
    if (in_vld)  in_cnt  <= in_cnt  + 3'd1;
    if (out_vld) out_cnt <= out_cnt + 3'd1;
  end

property p_data_in_order;
  logic [2:0] tkt;
  logic [7:0] exp;
  @(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n)
    (in_vld, tkt = in_cnt, exp = in_data)
      |=> (out_vld && out_cnt == tkt)[->1] ##0 (out_data == exp);
endproperty
a_data_in_order: assert property (p_data_in_order);
```

The sampled `in_cnt` at a byte's input edge is the number of bytes accepted before it; the sampled `out_cnt` at an output edge is the number delivered before it. Reset the counters with the same signal as the `disable iff`, so a reset clears both together.

Details interviewers probe: `##1` instead of `##0` checks the data one tick after the handshake; `[=1]` instead of `[->1]` lets a later tick's correct value excuse a wrong value on the handshake tick. If the design completes **out of order** by ID, keep one ticket counter per ID or move the check to a scoreboard: a property per ID multiplies attempts, and the scoreboard is cheaper to debug at that point.
